.TH PSMORE 1 "2026-08-02" "psmore" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
psmore \- relationship-first process diagnostics for macOS and Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B psmore
.RI [ QUERY ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B psmore
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B psmore
is an interactive process tree and a scriptable diagnostics workbench. It keeps
parent, child, sibling, service-subtree, command-line, resource, network, file,
and lifecycle evidence in one model. The default mode opens a terminal UI;
subcommands provide stable tables and versioned JSON for SSH sessions, incident
records, and health gates.
.PP
Collection is read-only unless the user explicitly invokes a process action in
the TUI. Visibility is limited by operating-system permissions. Missing evidence
is reported as incomplete or inconclusive rather than treated as proof that a
condition is absent.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check QUERY
Evaluate whether a process query has any or no matches. The collector process
and its contribution to ancestor subtree metrics are excluded from the gate.
Use
.B --wait
to retry until a condition converges and
.B --stable
to require consecutive passing evaluations.
.TP
.B inspect PID
Inspect one process, its runtime context, threads, sockets, files, limits, and
security evidence.
.TP
.B memory PID
Attribute one process instance's memory. Linux reports precise RSS, PSS,
anonymous, file, shared, private, swap, virtual-region, and mapped-file
evidence. macOS reports physical footprint and vmmap resident, dirty, and
swapped categories. Use
.B --limit
to bound category and mapped-file rows per section.
.TP
.B explain PID
Build one process dossier by collecting inspection, service-manager,
executable-image, and bounded native-log evidence in parallel. Rank review
signals by severity while retaining each source report and its coverage. It
also reports conservative CPU-sample, thread/socket volume, open-file
soft-limit, and Linux systemd TasksMax/MemoryMax pressure signals when the
required evidence is visible. A sampled CPU signal is not treated as sustained
load.
Use
.B --no-logs
or
.B --no-hash
for a faster metadata-only dossier, and
.B --output
to create a private JSON incident artifact.
.TP
.B exe PID
Compare the executable image held by a process with the current disk path.
Report replaced, deleted, missing, or matching image evidence, optional SHA-256,
package ownership, and macOS code-signing provenance. Use
.B --no-hash
to skip file hashing while retaining identity, package, and signing evidence.
.TP
.B stale
On Linux, scan visible processes for executable images that were deleted,
replaced at their original path, or whose disk path disappeared. Results retain
service and package ownership. Use
.B --expect none
as a conservative post-deployment gate; zero matches with incomplete visibility
are inconclusive rather than clean.
.TP
.B service PID
Resolve a process to its Linux systemd unit or macOS launchd job. Report
manager state, restart evidence, configuration origin, resource context, and
read-only suggested commands without changing the service.
.TP
.B logs PID
Read a bounded window of native logs for one process instance or its service.
Linux reads journald and can correlate a PID with its systemd unit; macOS reads
Unified Logging for the exact PID. Collection is read-only and revalidates the
process identity. Use
.BR --scope " auto|process|service" ,
.BR --since " DURATION" ,
.BR --priority " error|warning|info|debug" ,
and
.BR --limit " COUNT"
to control the evidence boundary.
.TP
.B port PORT
Find processes and socket references associated with a local TCP or UDP port.
.TP
.B listen
Inventory listening sockets, bind exposure, owners, and namespaces.
.TP
.B net
Search listening, connected, peer, and Unix socket evidence.
.TP
.B tree PID
Print a stable ancestor chain and nested descendants for one process.
.TP
.B watch
Stream starts, exits, PID reuse, reparenting, and query membership changes.
.TP
.B trace PID
Sample process and service-subtree CPU, memory, and I/O over time.
.TP
.B run [OPTIONS] -- COMMAND [ARGUMENTS]
Launch COMMAND and profile every observable process in its descendant tree.
The command inherits standard input, output, and error; the final profile is
written to the diagnostic standard error stream so command output remains safe
for pipelines. The psmore exit status mirrors COMMAND. Use
.B --linger-ms
to bound post-exit descendant observation. Use
.B --output
to atomically write an unambiguous private JSON report while preserving command
standard error.
.TP
.B deleted
Find deleted files that remain open and estimate reclaimable space.
.TP
.B file PATH
Find processes executing, mapping, opening, or using PATH as their current or
root directory. Existing targets are also matched by device and inode. Use
.B --recursive
to include every descendant of a directory or mount point; use
.B --expect none
as a conservative pre-replacement or pre-unmount safety gate.
.TP
.B fd
Rank file-descriptor pressure and compare counts with known process limits.
.TP
.B top
Rank process or complete subtree CPU, memory, read, and write hotspots.
.TP
.B oom
On Linux, correlate OOM selection priority with memory, swap, PSI, cgroup, and
kernel OOM evidence.
.TP
.B cgroup
On Linux, group visible processes by their actual leaf cgroup and correlate
sampled member CPU, RSS, and I/O with hierarchical memory, PID limits, and OOM
events from the kernel controllers. Filters search systemd, container, path,
and process context.
.TP
.B doctor
Run conservative host and process triage. With
.BR --deep ,
also scan network exposure, FD pressure, deleted-open files, and Linux OOM/PSI.
.TP
.B diff BEFORE AFTER
Compare two process snapshots or two host doctor reports. Doctor reports may use
.B --fail-on regression
as an automation gate.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Write a completion script to standard output.
.SH PROCESS QUERY
Plain terms search names and command context case-insensitively. Structured
fields include
.BR name: ,
.BR cmd: ,
.BR path: ,
.BR user: ,
.BR state: ,
.BR pid: ,
and
.BR ppid: .
Numeric conditions include
.BR cpu> ,
.BR mem>= ,
.BR read> ,
.BR write> ,
.BR age> ,
.BR children>= ,
.BR tree.cpu> ,
.BR tree.mem> ,
and
.BR tree.procs>= .
Prefix a condition with
.B !
to exclude it. Memory and I/O units accept k, m, g, and t; age accepts s, m, h,
and d.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -q , " --query " QUERY
Apply a process query.
.TP
.B --table
Write a stable human-readable table and exit.
.TP
.B --json
Write a versioned machine-readable JSON document and exit.
.TP
.B --redact
Mask common secret flags, assignments, headers, URLs, and query values in
command lines. Always review output before sharing it.
.TP
.BI --sample-ms " MILLISECONDS"
Set the resource sampling interval where supported.
.TP
.BI --scope " auto|process|service"
For logs and explain, choose an exact process instance, an owning systemd unit,
or automatic selection. Automatic mode broadens only to an actual .service, not
a generic login scope. macOS supports process and auto scope but refuses service
scope.
.TP
.BI --since " DURATION"
For logs and explain, select a recent window from 1 second through 7 days.
Durations accept s, m, h, or d. Process-scoped windows are clamped to the target
start time.
.TP
.BI --priority " error|warning|info|debug"
For logs and explain, select the maximum native-log verbosity. The default is
info.
.TP
.B --no-logs
For explain, omit the native-log section while retaining process, manager, and
executable evidence. It cannot be combined with log scope, time, priority, or
limit options.
.TP
.B --no-hash
For exe and explain, skip executable SHA-256 reads while retaining file
identity, package, and signing evidence.
.TP
.BI --wait " DURATION"
For
.BR check ,
retry until the policy passes or the timeout expires. Durations accept ms, s,
m, or h and must be at least as long as the sampling interval.
.TP
.BI --interval-ms " MILLISECONDS"
For check, set the evaluation cadence while waiting. For run, set the command
subtree sampling interval.
.TP
.BI --linger-ms " MILLISECONDS"
For run, continue observing known descendants for this many milliseconds after
the launched command exits. The default is 1000; the accepted range is 0 to
60000.
.TP
.BI --output " FILE"
Atomically write a mode-0600 JSON report for commands that support private
artifacts, including explain, run, doctor, and diff. Existing files are refused
unless
.B --force
is also specified.
.TP
.BI --stable " COUNT"
For
.BR check ,
require COUNT consecutive passing evaluations. A failing evaluation resets the
consecutive count.
.TP
.B --recursive
For
.BR file ,
match the resolved path and all descendants using component-aware path
comparison.
.TP
.BI --limit " COUNT|all"
Limit returned evidence rows for commands that support it. Policy evaluation is
performed before truncation.
.TP
.BI --expect " none|any"
Apply a policy to all matches. Confirmed violations exit 3. A zero-match result
with incomplete collection is inconclusive and exits 1.
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress normal output for a command with an explicit policy, using only the
exit status.
.TP
.B --no-tips
Suppress startup guidance for this TUI run without changing saved preferences.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show global or command-specific help.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the psmore version.
.SH TUI KEYS
.TP
.BR Up / Down
Move the selection. The viewport follows the selected row.
.TP
.BR Left / Right
Reveal parent context or expand/collapse the selected process subtree.
.TP
.B 0-9
Start direct PID entry. Enter locates the exact process and restores the full
tree with its ancestor path exposed; Backspace edits and Escape cancels.
.TP
.B /
Enter search input without changing the current tree. While editing, all
printable characters including j and k belong to the query. Enter applies the
query and returns to selection mode; k can then act on the selected result.
Escape cancels input, or clears an already active search.
.TP
.B Space
Pause or resume live refresh.
.TP
.B k
Open the focused end-process dialog for the selected process. Choose graceful
TERM or immediate KILL, review the target, then press y on the second screen to
send the signal. The PID start time is revalidated before every action.
.TP
.B D
Build a process dossier for the selected process in the background. The panel
prioritizes signals from deep inspection, service ownership, executable-image,
and bounded native-log evidence. Enter or r refreshes; i, M, m, v, and l open
related evidence; L toggles log collection; h toggles SHA-256; D or Escape
closes the workspace.
.TP
.B M
Open memory attribution for the selected process in the background. Linux
separates RSS, PSS, anonymous/file/shared pages, swap, virtual categories, and
mapped files; macOS separates physical footprint from shared-inclusive vmmap
region totals. Enter or r refreshes; D opens the process dossier; M or Escape
closes the workspace.
.TP
.B m
Open the selected process service-context workspace. Resolve the owning systemd
unit or launchd job in the background and show state, configuration, resource
boundaries, and suggested read-only commands. Linux preserves finite,
unlimited, and unavailable TasksMax and MemoryMax states separately. Enter or r
refreshes; m or Escape closes the workspace.
.TP
.B v
Open the selected process executable-image workspace. Verify image identity,
disk drift, package ownership, hashes, and signing in the background. Enter or
r refreshes, h toggles SHA-256 collection, m switches to service context, l
switches to native logs, and v or Escape closes the workspace.
.TP
.B l
Open bounded native logs for the selected process in the background. Enter or r
refreshes; s cycles auto, process, and service scope; p changes the maximum
priority; w changes the time window; m and v switch to related context; l or
Escape closes the workspace.
.TP
.BR n ,
.BR d ,
.BR b ,
and
.B i
Open network, deep inspection, baseline/diff, or process detail workspaces.
.TP
.BR ?
Open the field guide. Within guidance,
.B T
toggles future tips and
.B D
disables startup cards.
.TP
.BR q
Close the current panel or quit from the main process tree.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed and any requested policy passed.
.TP
.B 1
Collection or runtime failed, or evidence was insufficient for a conclusive
policy result.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage, input schema, or unsafe output selection was invalid.
.TP
.B 3
A confirmed expectation, severity threshold, or regression policy failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/psmore/ui-state.json
Default macOS TUI preference state.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/psmore/ui-state.json
Default Linux TUI preference state.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/psmore/uninstall.sh
Uninstaller for a binary-package installation. It does not remove TUI state or
diagnostic reports.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PSMORE_CONFIG_DIR
Override the directory used for private TUI preference state.
.TP
.B PSMORE_PREFIX
Override the binary package install prefix. The default is
.IR ~/.local .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Select the Linux user configuration root when
.B PSMORE_CONFIG_DIR
is unset.
.SH PRIVACY AND SAFETY
Process arguments, paths, usernames, hostnames, sockets, and files may contain
sensitive data. JSON exports are created privately when written through psmore,
but redaction is best effort. Review reports before sharing them. Run with only
the privileges needed for the incident; higher privileges increase visibility
and the impact of explicit process actions.
.PP
The
.B file
command is read-only. Linux reads executable, cwd, root, descriptor, and mapping
evidence from
.IR /proc ;
macOS performs one global
.BR lsof (8)
scan. psmore and its collection helper are excluded. Incomplete visibility is
reported explicitly and never used to prove that a path has no users.
.PP
The
.B memory
command is read-only and revalidates PID identity after collection. Linux
reads
.IR /proc/PID/smaps_rollup ,
.IR /proc/PID/status ,
and
.IR /proc/PID/maps .
Its category and mapped-file byte counts are virtual address ranges, not
resident attribution. macOS uses
.BR vmmap (1)
summary mode; physical footprint and region resident bytes are reported as
different measures because region totals include shared mappings. Missing or
permission-limited evidence remains explicit. The versioned
.B psmore.process-memory
report can contain commands, users, memory layout, and mapped paths.
.PP
The
.B explain
command runs the process inspection, service-manager, executable-image, and
native-log collectors in parallel. It validates every nested PID and start time
against one initial process identity and refreshes the target after collection;
confirmed PID reuse is refused. Partial evidence remains visible and is never
treated as proof of absence. Ranked signals are review priorities with stable
codes and JSON evidence paths, not asserted root causes. Open-file pressure
uses visible descriptor counts against RLIMIT_NOFILE; systemd task and memory
pressure signals use TasksCurrent/TasksMax and MemoryCurrent/MemoryMax. The
versioned
.B psmore.process-dossier
report can contain all sensitive fields from its source reports.
.PP
The
.B exe
command is read-only. Linux compares
.I /proc/PID/exe
device and inode evidence with the current disk path and can hash each image up
to a 1 GiB safety limit. macOS reports current-path package and code-signing
evidence while explicitly noting that independent mapped-image identity is not
available. PID identity is revalidated after collection.
.PP
The
.B stale
command performs the same Linux mapped-image identity comparison across all
query-eligible processes. Result truncation does not affect policy evaluation.
Unreadable or racing processes make a zero-match policy inconclusive. Suggested
restart review and per-PID verification commands are printed but not executed.
.PP
The
.B service
command is also read-only. Linux derives membership from
.I /proc/PID/cgroup
and queries machine-readable systemd properties. macOS maps the process ancestor
chain through the current launchd bootstrap namespace and only emits an
allowlist of job fields; launchd environment dictionaries are not copied into
the report. Suggested commands are printed, not executed.
.PP
The
.B logs
command bounds both its time window and retained entry count. Linux derives a
systemd selector from
.I /proc/PID/cgroup
and reads journal JSON. Process scope is clamped to the current PID start time;
service scope can include earlier restart generations. macOS uses
.B log show
for the exact PID and refuses unsupported service-wide attribution. Log messages
may contain business data or secrets; redaction is best effort and output must
be reviewed before sharing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR lsof (8),
.BR top (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR log (1)
